# AscendNPU-IR Development Skill (Cursor)

Use this rule when working on `Ascend/AscendNPU-IR` code, features, or bugfixes.

## 1) Repository mental model

- `bishengir/include/bishengir/**`: dialect/conversion/pass declarations, `.td` TableGen defs, public APIs.
- `bishengir/lib/**`: implementations of dialects, conversions, passes, transforms, tools internals.
- `bishengir/tools/**`: CLI drivers (`bishengir-compile`, `bishengir-opt`, LSP/tool generators).
- `bishengir/test/**`: lit tests, tool tests, conversion/dialect/pass tests, Integration E2E.
- `bishengir/python/**`: Python bindings and wheel packaging.
- `build-tools/**`: build automation scripts (`build.sh`, `apply_patches.sh`, `build_wheel.sh`).
- `docs/source/en/**` + `docs/source/zh_cn/**`: official user/developer/contribution documentation.
- `third-party/**`: llvm-project / torch-mlir source dependencies.

## 2) Compiler stack and roles

- Entry stack: Torch-MLIR / Triton / TileLang / framework IR.
- Mid-level: Linalg/Tensor + `HFusion` (fusion, scheduling, hardware-aware optimizations).
- Low-level: `HIVM` (instruction-level mapping, memory/pipeline/sync control).
- Backend handoff: low-level IR to downstream LLVM/lower binary generation path.

## 3) Where to change code by task

- New/extended op semantics:
  - Add/update `.td` and headers in `include/bishengir/Dialect/<Dialect>/...`
  - Add/update C++ impl in `lib/Dialect/<Dialect>/...`
- New lowering path:
  - Add conversion in `include/bishengir/Conversion/...` and `lib/Conversion/...`
- New optimization pass:
  - Define/register pass in `include/bishengir/Pass` or dialect transform headers
  - Implement in `lib/Pass` or `lib/Dialect/<Dialect>/Transforms`
- CLI/flag behavior:
  - `tools/bishengir-compile` and/or `tools/bishengir-opt`
  - Ensure docs update in `docs/source/en/user_guide/compile_option.md`
- Custom operator behavior:
  - Follow CustomOp model in HIVM path and related lowering/attributes handling
  - Validate pass interactions (flatten, alignment, memory planning, layout transforms)

## 4) Build, test, and debug workflow

1. Initialize dependencies:
   - `git submodule update --init --recursive`
2. First build (recommended script):
   - `./build-tools/build.sh -o ./build --build-type Release --apply-patches`
3. Incremental build:
   - `./build-tools/build.sh -o ./build --build-type Release`
4. Run tests:
   - `ninja check-bishengir` (in build dir), or
   - `cmake --build . --target "check-mlir;check-bishengir"`
5. Focused pass debug:
   - `bishengir-opt input.mlir --<pass-name>`
6. IR inspection:
   - `bishengir-compile ... --bishengir-print-ir-before=<pass> --bishengir-print-ir-after=<pass>`

## 5) Feature-specific guidance

- HFusion AutoSchedule:
  - Focus on `lib/Dialect/HFusion/Transforms/AutoSchedule/**`
  - Respect alignment/tiling constraints and fusion-kind strategy selection.
- CV optimization/CVPipeline:
  - Validate cube/vector dependency legality, pipeline stage count, and buffering overhead.
- Debug instrumentation:
  - HFusion `print` and HIVM `debug` ops are first-line diagnostics for kernel issues.

## 6) Documentation and quality gates

- Keep docs aligned with behavior changes:
  - Architecture/user options/developer guide pages under `docs/source/en/**` (and zh counterpart if needed).
- Preserve LLVM style and existing conventions (`.clang-format`, `.clang-tidy`).
- Keep changes scoped: avoid unrelated refactors in PRs.
